WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.402
-Our next guest is an
Academy Award-winning actor

00:00:03.402 --> 00:00:05.371
you know from iconic films like
"Thelma &amp; Louise,

00:00:05.371 --> 00:00:07.139
"A League of Their Own,"
and "Beetlejuice."

00:00:07.139 --> 00:00:08.775
She's also the founder
and chair of the

00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:11.510
Geena Davis Institute
on Gender in Media.

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:12.879
Her memoir,
"Dying of Politeness,"

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:13.979
is out now.

00:00:13.979 --> 00:00:15.014
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:15.014 --> 00:00:17.483
our friend Geena Davis,
everyone.

00:00:17.483 --> 00:00:19.486
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:38.504
Hi, Geena.
I'm so happy to have you here.

00:00:38.504 --> 00:00:40.640
-I'm so happy to be here.
Thank you.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.175
-We ran into each other at
the Emmys, which was lovely.

00:00:43.175 --> 00:00:44.410
-Yes, yes.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:46.846
-But you got one selfie
from the Emmys,

00:00:46.846 --> 00:00:50.182
and I want to point out,
it was not with me.

00:00:50.182 --> 00:00:54.821
And I also saw Lizzo.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:54.821 --> 00:00:56.789
And I feel like
I blew my opportunity

00:00:56.789 --> 00:00:57.823
to get a selfie with her.

00:00:57.823 --> 00:00:59.658
You even gave her
a shout-out onstage.

00:00:59.658 --> 00:01:01.094
-Well, that's why
she wanted a selfie.

00:01:01.094 --> 00:01:02.662
-Okay, got it.
-Because I gave her a shout-out.

00:01:02.662 --> 00:01:08.000
No, well, yeah, because she --
Her award was right before me,

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.469
and she was talking about
the same kinds of stuff

00:01:10.469 --> 00:01:12.105
that I got the award for.

00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:13.739
-Which was about representation.

00:01:13.739 --> 00:01:15.941
And, you know, again,
you talk --

00:01:15.941 --> 00:01:17.911
Your whole institution is
about, you know,

00:01:17.911 --> 00:01:19.545
gender in media
and representation.

00:01:19.545 --> 00:01:22.648
And it's actually
very fitting to have you here

00:01:22.648 --> 00:01:24.516
the same night as
the vice president,

00:01:24.516 --> 00:01:26.518
because you played
the president on television.

00:01:26.518 --> 00:01:28.721
-Oh, right. That's right.

00:01:28.721 --> 00:01:33.559
-Fictional you,
one step above the old...

00:01:33.559 --> 00:01:35.527
-You know, I didn't say
anything about that

00:01:35.527 --> 00:01:36.662
when I met her backstage.

00:01:36.662 --> 00:01:38.297
-That's very cool.
That's very...

00:01:38.297 --> 00:01:40.433
But you were on a show called
"Commander in Chief,"

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:41.901
and this was only on
for one season,

00:01:41.901 --> 00:01:45.171
but it did -- it did
make a difference.

00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:46.472
-It did.

00:01:46.472 --> 00:01:50.343
A survey was done after
the show went off the air

00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:54.581
and found that people were 58%
more likely to vote for

00:01:54.581 --> 00:01:56.515
a female candidate for president

00:01:56.515 --> 00:01:59.351
after seeing me
behind the desk only 19 times.

00:01:59.351 --> 00:02:00.586
-Yeah.
-So...

00:02:00.586 --> 00:02:02.922
-I mean, imagine if it had
been on a second season.

00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:07.527
-I would -- I would
be the president by now.

00:02:07.527 --> 00:02:10.396
-Congratulations on your book.
-Thank you.

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:14.500
-I heard that you kept
index cards for both book ideas,

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:15.601
remembering things,

00:02:15.601 --> 00:02:17.469
but also inventions.

00:02:17.469 --> 00:02:19.472
You've come up with
some ideas for inventions.

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:21.173
-I -- Yeah. I can't help it.

00:02:21.173 --> 00:02:24.376
I just constantly come up
with ideas for stupid things.

00:02:24.376 --> 00:02:27.913
When I was shooting archery,
I would tell my coach excitedly,

00:02:27.913 --> 00:02:31.216
you know, "I came up with this
great thing that archers need."

00:02:31.216 --> 00:02:32.551
And he said, "Oh, that's great.

00:02:32.551 --> 00:02:35.221
You'll make tens of dollars."

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:36.922
-Well, you have -- Can you share
your fish-tank idea?

00:02:36.922 --> 00:02:38.157
-Yes, yes.

00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:41.661
So, it's --
It would be home snorkeling.

00:02:41.661 --> 00:02:42.829
-Yeah.

00:02:42.829 --> 00:02:45.431
-And, so, if you had
a very large fish tank

00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:48.067
and it has
a gasket on the bottom

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:52.138
so you can poke your head up
and put your mask on,

00:02:52.138 --> 00:02:54.106
and then snorkel
in your own fish tank.

00:02:54.106 --> 00:02:59.145
-Gotcha. So, from the neck up,
you'd be snorkeling?

00:02:59.145 --> 00:03:00.546
-Yes.
-Wow.

00:03:00.546 --> 00:03:01.914
-Who said you need your body
to be snorkeling?

00:03:01.914 --> 00:03:04.483
-Yeah, no one ever said
one way or the other.

00:03:04.483 --> 00:03:05.918
Yeah, I mean --
-Right.

00:03:05.918 --> 00:03:08.254
-I do -- If you ever are
looking for investors,

00:03:08.254 --> 00:03:10.590
please make sure I don't
accidentally give you money.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.091
[ Laughter ]

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:14.160
-Rats!

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.362
-So, the title
"Dying of Politeness"

00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:19.699
is based on a story
from your youth.

00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:24.637
-Yes, yes. When I literally
almost died of politeness.

00:03:24.637 --> 00:03:27.874
When I was about 8 or so,
my parents and I

00:03:27.874 --> 00:03:31.711
went out to dinner with
my great-uncle And great-aunt.

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:35.447
And Uncle Jack was
driving us back to his house,

00:03:35.447 --> 00:03:38.117
who's 99 years old.

00:03:38.117 --> 00:03:41.020
And, so, it was
a quiet little street,

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:44.991
but he kind of veered in
the oncoming lane and back.

00:03:44.991 --> 00:03:48.661
And my parents realized
this was going on.

00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:50.462
And I was sitting
right behind him,

00:03:50.462 --> 00:03:52.298
and they put me in between them

00:03:52.298 --> 00:03:55.535
because I would die
a little less.

00:03:56.869 --> 00:03:58.437
Forget about that I had
a straight shot

00:03:58.437 --> 00:04:00.873
through the windshield.

00:04:00.873 --> 00:04:02.441
So, finally,
he veers over there,

00:04:02.441 --> 00:04:06.145
and there's nowhere to --
It's a two-lane road.

00:04:06.145 --> 00:04:08.280
And he stays
straddling the line.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:11.550
And a car is coming,
and we're going to be killed.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:14.087
And we're gonna have
a head-on collision.

00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:16.355
And my parents say nothing.

00:04:16.355 --> 00:04:20.459
And we're a millisecond
away from, you know,

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:24.730
having this accident, and,
finally, my great-aunt says,

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:26.899
"A little to the right, Jack."

00:04:29.268 --> 00:04:32.604
He goes a little to the right,
and then I see the faces --

00:04:32.604 --> 00:04:34.507
The people are passing by
about a foot away,

00:04:34.507 --> 00:04:38.177
and I see their
horrified faces streaking past.

00:04:38.177 --> 00:04:41.080
-But this was your parents
would rather die of politeness

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:42.681
than say anything to Uncle Jack.

00:04:42.681 --> 00:04:45.884
-Well, you know,
later that I realized

00:04:45.884 --> 00:04:50.323
we were gonna --
They were willing to die --

00:04:50.323 --> 00:04:53.025
literally die --
rather than say something.

00:04:53.025 --> 00:04:55.761
I mean, but they could have
politely also said,

00:04:55.761 --> 00:04:57.062
"A little to the right, Jack,"

00:04:57.062 --> 00:05:01.367
instead of, "Holy God,
you're gonna kill us all.

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:04.570
"Pull over.
Let me drive," you know?

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:07.239
Where was "protect the child"
part? Yeah.

00:05:07.239 --> 00:05:11.176
-Well, it's very damning of
that style of back-seat driving.

00:05:11.176 --> 00:05:12.578
-Right, right, right.

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:14.613
-You talk about
"Thelma &amp; Louise"

00:05:14.613 --> 00:05:18.050
and also that you were
in this sort of brief moment

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:20.219
where people thought,
from "Thelma &amp; Louise" and from

00:05:20.219 --> 00:05:21.453
"A League of Their Own,"

00:05:21.453 --> 00:05:23.823
that it was
the beginning of a movement,

00:05:23.823 --> 00:05:25.357
that, all of a sudden,
there would be these

00:05:25.357 --> 00:05:28.360
female-driven dramas,
female-driven comedies,

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:31.097
and then it
kind of didn't happen.

00:05:31.097 --> 00:05:32.431
-Did not happen. Right.

00:05:32.431 --> 00:05:35.668
And female sports movies --
that was the big thing.

00:05:35.668 --> 00:05:37.236
Because, yeah,
they're always a big hit.

00:05:37.236 --> 00:05:40.406
And, yeah, both of those movies,
the press announced,

00:05:40.406 --> 00:05:42.407
"This will now
change everything."

00:05:42.407 --> 00:05:44.577
And waiting for that.

00:05:44.577 --> 00:05:48.214
And I'm in two movies
that changed everything.

00:05:49.881 --> 00:05:53.886
And maybe
five years or so later,

00:05:53.886 --> 00:05:55.688
"First Wives Club" comes out.

00:05:55.688 --> 00:05:57.623
-Yeah.
-And that was a big hit.

00:05:57.623 --> 00:06:00.826
And "Okay, now this one will
change everything."

00:06:00.826 --> 00:06:03.629
And so every, you know,
five-or-so years,

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:07.766
something comes along that
again doesn't change everything.

00:06:07.766 --> 00:06:09.402
-I think it's always a mistake

00:06:09.402 --> 00:06:12.304
to think one is
an indicator of a pattern.

00:06:12.304 --> 00:06:13.739
-Exactly, exactly.
-Yeah.

00:06:13.739 --> 00:06:15.808
-To be on, like,
"People" magazine cover,

00:06:15.808 --> 00:06:17.110
it has to have happened
three times.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:18.343
-Yeah.

00:06:18.343 --> 00:06:19.912
-Because then it's --
Yeah, now it's a thing.

00:06:19.912 --> 00:06:21.714
-Then it's an actual thing.
-Then it's a thing.

00:06:21.714 --> 00:06:25.184
-You are an Oscar winner,
and, yet, by this photo,

00:06:25.184 --> 00:06:28.087
one of your Oscars
had a bit of a mishap.

00:06:28.087 --> 00:06:29.254
-Yes.

00:06:29.254 --> 00:06:32.791
-Before I show this photo,
what happened?

00:06:32.791 --> 00:06:37.196
-So, it was the
'92 earthquake in California,

00:06:37.196 --> 00:06:41.567
and it fell off the mantel
and got dented.

00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:43.669
And I asked them,
could they repair it,

00:06:43.669 --> 00:06:45.371
and then they said "No,
we'll give you a different one."

00:06:45.371 --> 00:06:47.606
"No, no, no.
I'll keep the real one."

00:06:47.606 --> 00:06:50.076
-See if you can guess
which one fell.

00:06:52.511 --> 00:06:55.281
What I like about it is,
you could also say

00:06:55.281 --> 00:06:59.218
this one's bent or this one
has really bad B.O.

00:06:59.218 --> 00:07:00.319
-Oh!
-Yeah.

00:07:00.319 --> 00:07:01.687
-That's good.

00:07:01.687 --> 00:07:04.156
-This one is opting out of where
you put him on the mantel.

00:07:04.156 --> 00:07:06.592
-"Dude."
-"Dude."

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:08.060
Congratulations on the book,

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:10.162
and thank you so much
for being here with us again.

00:07:10.162 --> 00:07:11.397
It's always such a pleasure.
-Thank you.

00:07:11.397 --> 00:07:12.899
-Geena Davis, everybody.

00:07:12.899 --> 00:07:14.967
"Dying of Politeness"
is available now.

00:07:14.967 --> 00:07:16.536
We'll be back with more
"Late Night."

